.he "halt (1)                     9/17/26                    halt (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
halt
- stop the machine
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
halt [ -f ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
With no argument, halt sends init a terminate signal, and init shuts
the machine down: every process is killed and logged out, the file
systems are unmounted, a message is written on the console, and the
machine halts.
.sp
With
.bd
-f,
halt calls the reboot system call directly, which flushes the buffer
pool and drops the cpu with none of init's grace.  Use it when the
system is too sick to shut down the long way.
.sp
Only the super-user may halt the machine.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
init (1), reboot (2), sync (1)
